            0.117
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "司"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.702,
            0.078,
            0.868,
            0.094
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.09,
            0.379,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.121,
            0.61,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why do classification systems keep changing? Does that indicate how science works? Yes, science evolves and progresses as we learn new things. For example, Aristotle’s system worked for his time, but with new tools (like microscopes) and technological advancements (like staining techniques), scientists discovered microorganisms. These discoveries helped us understand the natural world better. Accordingly, the biological classification changed. The biological classification (and science) thus, is an ongoing process of reasoning and change."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.307,
            0.415,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.9 Fossils as Evidence"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.332,
            0.641,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we talk of diversity changing over time, what evidence do we have? Fossils are preserved remains of plants and animals found in layers of rocks, sand and mud. Generally, older layers contain simpler organisms, while newer layers show more complex forms. From giant dinosaurs to early humans and ancient plants, some important fossils have been discovered in India. Fossils act as natural records that help us understand how life has changed over millions of years."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.497,
            0.522,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.10 Biodiversity Under Threat"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.522,
            0.641,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Each species, large or small, plays an important role in nature. Plants produce food and oxygen, animals pollinate flowers and disperse seeds, and microorganisms recycle nutrients. Human activities, such as pollution, deforestation, the overuse of resources and climate change are reducing biodiversity. When one species disappears, others that depend on it may also decline in number and eventually also, disappear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.685,
            0.17,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.112,
                0.685,
                0.17,
                0.716
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_19_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.689,
            0.392,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.72,
            0.645,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When scientists discover a new organism, they compare it with the already known species. Sometimes, the species name reflects their place of discovery, or honours a scientist. The Purple Frog (Nasikabatrachus sahyadrensis) from Kerala has a species name after the Sahyadri Hills (Fig. 12.20)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.8,
            0.646,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Purple Frog lives underground for most of the year and comes out only during the monsoon to breed. It belongs to an ancient family of frogs as understood from fossils. Its discovery in 2003 helped scientists understand ancient amphibian groups and highlighted the need for biodiversity conservation in the Western Ghats."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.701,
            0.106,
            0.789,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.701,
                0.106,
                0.789,
                0.183
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_19_image_14.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.799,
            0.108,
            0.932,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Birbal Sahni",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was an eminent scientist who studied fossil plants. He"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.692,
            0.185,
            0.939,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "founded the Birbal Sahni Institute of Palaeosciences (BSIP) in Lucknow, which continues his work on ancient plants and past environments even to this day. His studies helped link present-day plants with their ancestors and showed that life on the Earth has a long, connected history. His work continues to inspire young scientists to explore how fossils reveal the story of our planet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.672,
            0.427,
            0.918,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.687,
            0.457,
            0.938,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Does the term ‘biodiversity’ relate only to the variety of organisms, or does it encompass other elements?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.688,
            0.521,
            0.939,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. If you find a new organism in a pond, what features will you observe to classify it and why?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.688,
            0.583,
            0.921,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Why do genetic studies provide deep information about living beings?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.631,
            0.876,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10. How can changes in climate affect the biodiversity?"
            }
          ]
        },
